# CloudSentinel — Docker build context exclusions.
#
# What this is: the list of paths Docker must NOT send to the daemon when
# building an image. Everything not excluded here is uploaded as the build
# context and is a candidate for ending up inside an image layer.
#
# Where it fits: it guards the boundary between the working tree and the
# container images defined in Dockerfile. Nothing else reads it.
#
# This file has two jobs, and the first one is security.
#
# 1. SECURITY — keep secrets out of image layers.
#
#    An image layer is immutable and public once pushed. Deleting a file in a
#    later `RUN` does not remove it from the layer that added it: `docker
#    history` and any layer-extraction tool will still recover it. So a secret
#    that enters the build context can never be taken back out of an image that
#    copied it.
#
#    `.env` holds POSTGRES_PASSWORD and CLOUDSENTINEL_JWT_SECRET — the JWT
#    secret being the single most sensitive value in the project, since anyone
#    holding it can forge a token claiming to be any user with any role. The
#    Dockerfile uses `COPY . .`, so without the rule below that file would be
#    baked into every build made on this machine. Configuration reaches the
#    containers at *runtime* instead, from a Kubernetes Secret.
#
#    Note that `.env*` also covers `.env.local`, `.env.production` and the rest
#    of the family, while `!.env.example` lets the committed no-values template
#    through so the image documents what it expects.
#
# 2. Size and cache stability — keep the context small.
#
#    Every file in the context is hashed on every build. `node_modules/` alone
#    is hundreds of megabytes that `npm ci` reproduces exactly from
#    package-lock.json anyway, and `ml/.venv/` is a machine-specific tree of
#    compiled wheels that would not even run on the Linux base image. Including
#    either would slow every build and invalidate the cache constantly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: credentials and keys. Keep this block first — it is the one that
# matters if the rest of the file is ever mangled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
k8s/tls/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependency trees — reinstalled inside the image from their lockfiles.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules
ml/.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output. The image builds its own; copying a Windows-built .next into a
# Linux image is how a stale or platform-wrong bundle gets shipped.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.next
out
build
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control and editor state. `.git` in particular is both large and a
# disclosure risk: it carries the full history, including any file that was
# committed and later removed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests and generated fixtures.
#
# The test suite runs in CI, not in the image, so shipping it only enlarges the
# attack surface of the running container for no benefit.
#
# The synthetic activity log is excluded for a different reason: it is derived
# data. `npm run logs:gen` rebuilds it deterministically in about 200ms, so a
# copy inside the image would be tens of megabytes of something the image can
# already produce — and a stale one at that.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/*.test.ts
**/*.dbtest.ts
fixtures/cloudtrail.json
fixtures/cloudtrail-labels.json
fixtures/anomalies.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation and local infrastructure definitions. The images do not read
# them, and a change to a README should not invalidate a build cache.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
README.md
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
